WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.710
- It is no cliche that if you
work for a consulting firm

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.707
you will likely create lots of slides.

00:00:04.707 --> 00:00:06.360
And so in the six years I worked

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.120
for the consulting firm, McKinsey,

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.427
I probably created really
thousands of PowerPoint pages.

00:00:11.427 --> 00:00:14.130
And so today's coffee
break here on my channel,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.000
I wanna share some additional
tips on exactly the topic

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.410
and specifically on
the thing that arguably

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.710
is both the most important
part of slide writing

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.730
but also the most challenging
part of slide writing

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.360
in the life of anyone working
in professional services.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.820
And this is creating charts and diagrams

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.560
that first, look professional

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.030
and then also, communicate your message

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:37.680
in an effective and concise
and to the point way.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.318
I'm very proud that this
video is sponsored think-cell.

00:00:40.318 --> 00:00:42.770
Think-cell that is a plugin
for PowerPoint and Excel

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.580
that is used by most of
the large consulting firms,

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.980
and I probably use it
literally every single day

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.107
but more about them in a moment.

00:00:50.107 --> 00:00:52.050
So, the plan for this
video is the following.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.480
First, I'm going to talk
about chart formatting.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.390
So, how to make sure

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.680
that your charts really look professional?

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.930
Second, I'm going to talk
about chart selection.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:01.800
One of the most important
things to get right

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.860
is really to choose the right
chart type for your data.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.980
And we're going to do this
with some little exercises.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.660
So, it's going to be very hands-on.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:09.800
And then last but not least,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.100
I'm going to talk about
some typical errors

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.540
that people employ when
they work with charts,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.970
with data in creating slides.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.950
So without further ado, let's get into it.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.750
Let's talk about chart formatting.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.890
So, let's look at this example of a chart.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.730
Let's just look at the formatting.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.130
So, how this looks.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.370
What do you think?

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.370
Is this chart looking professional?

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.320
Should you make a chart
that looks like this?

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.590
And feel free to pause maybe for a moment

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.000
and potentially think about
how you could improve this.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.970
So, take a couple of seconds if you like.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.420
And now, I wanna improve
this chart together with you.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.390
And the first thing that
likely comes to mind

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.660
is all these colors in the chart.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.210
So, should you really use
that many different colors

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.170
for all these different bars?

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:54.050
And the general idea is
that there's nothing wrong

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.000
with using colors in your chart.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.690
You should just make sure
that colors have a meaning.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:03.040
Colors should convey a
message, then you can use them.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.410
You shouldn't just use colors

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.350
for the sake of making
everything colorful.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.370
And so, here for this
chart I would recommend you

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:12.330
to rather use tone down
colors, very basic colors

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.610
because I'd argue all these colors here

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.490
don't really help you to
bring across any message.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.280
If at all, it is distracting
from what you wanna show.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.830
And then, maybe you can set an accent.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.273
A color accent on the latest
the year just to raise

00:02:25.273 --> 00:02:28.050
that this is the newest number
here in the time series.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.130
The latest figure that you maybe also

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:31.700
wanna focus on with your chart.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.360
The next thing that might come to mind

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.700
is all these lines that you see here.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.270
It just looks very crowded, very busy.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:39.620
Even though actually the numbers

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.600
that you're trying to show here
are quite basic and simple.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.720
So, probably the first
thing I wanna take out

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.770
are all these connector
lines between the bars.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.470
I'd argue they don't really add

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.520
any value at all here in this chart.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.250
And the same thing is likely also true

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.770
for all these helper lines,

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:57.020
all these grid lines in the background.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.110
And here I'd argue what I'd take out

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.740
is also the Y-axis to the side.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:02.573
In this example,

00:03:02.573 --> 00:03:06.310
you already have all the
numbers on top of the bars.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.630
Why do you need to show
this additional axis?

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:10.530
Take it out and you will be very fine.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.810
And now let's also look at the X-axis

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.740
'cause it comes with a very
bold, dominant dark black

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.750
that really dominates the whole picture.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.190
So, I'd say it's not really helpful

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.160
because of course you
want rather the numbers,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.730
the bars to stand out and
not this very bold X-axis.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:27.610
So, let's lighten it up a little bit.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.860
And now what I would encourage you to add

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.320
are rather some arrows, different arrows,

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.170
some elements that really help
you to bring out the message

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:37.290
that you wanna bring out.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.750
And of course, what
exactly that is will depend

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.440
on indeed the message that
you do wanna communicate.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.890
But maybe you wanna show
how the revenues increased

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.350
from 2015 to 2021.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.190
Maybe this is what you would want to show.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.770
And if this is indeed the case,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.860
why don't include a
different arrow that shows

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:59.467
how the revenue increased
by 400% from 2015 to 2021.

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.820
And then this will potentially be a number

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:06.350
that you could also cite and
refer to in the action title.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.090
So, if you don't know
what action titles are,

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.490
they're also very important
part of slide writing.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:11.810
I will link another video that it did

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.740
on this very topic above here.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.680
So, one key concept to have in mind here

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.860
is the idea of the data-to-ink ratio.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.730
The idea is that this ratio
should be as high as possible.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:25.840
So, ideally you're able to
show as much data as possible

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.340
with as little ink as possible.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:29.610
And the implication of this

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.250
is that you should just take out,

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:34.530
delete all the additional
filler lines, helper lines

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:37.230
background lines, axis,
and so on and so forth.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:40.290
Unless of course they're really
needed and really add value

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.560
to the message that
you wanna bring across.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.480
So, always think data-to-ink ratio

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.960
and this alone will already
improve your charts.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:47.810
I promise.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:50.020
Next, let's talk about
choosing the right chart type

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.900
for the data that you wanna visualize

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.060
because this indeed is of more complicated

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.080
than it might look.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.490
So, let's start with this
very simple exercise here.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.160
You see different products
and how much revenue

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.800
they contribute to the
total revenue of a company.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.170
So, the sum of them all
is a hundred percent.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:08.250
So, what chart type would
you choose to visualize this?

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.300
And again, feel free to
take a couple of seconds.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.930
Maybe pause the video to
think about this indeed.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:14.710
Now, let's look at a couple of examples

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:17.120
because at least from my
experience, the chart type

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.970
that people will mention most
frequently in this situation

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:21.490
is a pie chart.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.740
So, we should surely use a pie chart

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.507
to visualize this, right?

00:05:25.507 --> 00:05:27.690
If you are to directly see in this example

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.780
is that this pie chart looks
quite messy, quite overcrowded.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:33.910
The basic rule of thumb
is that whenever you have

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:36.610
more than about five elements to show,

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.310
pie charts are quite
difficult to work with

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.640
because the elements are
just getting too small

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.850
to really display them in
a visually appealing way.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:46.130
But then also at least if you
read the (mumbles) on that

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.410
just interpreting the area of an element

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.720
does not really seem to be too
intuitive to the human mind.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.670
So, is it really that clear
to you that for instance,

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.100
product nine is twice the share,

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.280
twice the size of product 10?

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.290
Or product five is twice
the size of product nine?

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:04.720
So, these relative differences in area

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.370
are not that natural to
grasp and to understand,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.610
and often bar charts like this
as shown here on the right

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.910
might be a bit more of an intuitive way

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:16.970
of also really comparing even
relative numbers like this.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:18.033
And the key point that I wanna make here

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:20.840
is that in the beginning
of visualizing data,

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.270
always make it an effort of yours,

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:24.620
so thinking about what
the options might be

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.160
and what really the best choice

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:27.500
of a chart might be for that.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.840
And of course, some chart
types just work better

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:32.380
for some specific types
of data than for others.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.590
So here, for instance,
a couple of archetypes.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:36.410
So, component comparison.

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.270
So, relative comparison.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.340
Here, pie charts or stacked column charts

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.660
might work quite well.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.560
So, for things of share
of, percentage of and so on

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.830
if it's more about an absolute comparison

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.820
then you maybe want to go
for the vertical bar charts

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:51.050
or the waterfall charts.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.130
For time series,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.800
of course line charts or
column charts might work.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:55.850
So, if we really wanna show

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:58.400
how a certain time series
develops over time.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.380
Then of course, they have distributions,

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:02.480
correlations and so on and so forth.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:04.080
I would actually
encourage you to print out

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.130
this specific slide
and potentially keep it

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:07.750
as a cheat sheet next to your desk.

